9addc518226169f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-4,-4),$ $a=4$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d6d789-9a13-55ed-9aa3-a6599922e6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404010} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0179db-bc3d-59ac-a7ab-f6e35d77cdda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} {:answer/id #uuid "22551caa-d93a-5d26-a64b-1d4d526c0551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404012} {:answer/id #uuid "61661834-8abe-5e9e-95ce-3916c77f8307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404013} {:answer/id #uuid "10b69a37-8e76-5d45-bf03-9dcbeccb702d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0179db-bc3d-59ac-a7ab-f6e35d77cdda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} :answer-field/id #uuid "6729ae77-9d58-5d6c-9396-7d1f8b6ef681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9082f86b-7c0f-5dd7-b074-5c0e7d067128" :question/math-academy-id "q-79287" :question/problem "![](images/07/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404017} {:answer/id #uuid "32f648c8-3d4d-551c-a0fe-e98c1f6d2eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404018} {:answer/id #uuid "89052cbe-3422-56ea-9b9a-942801acf80e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404019} {:answer/id #uuid "8b13ccd7-add3-5ba4-bf95-5440c51f18c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404020} {:answer/id #uuid "1c440c18-9297-5a46-a77e-a81bd6a4fec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404017} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9041eb-bfd0-524e-8602-d57198b3ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a6a1e67-ac65-5715-8684-1ea8d5d95e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-79292" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5102bd-f838-51a2-a43d-a2fd2a78f6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404024} {:answer/id #uuid "72c55eb1-bc22-5e56-b296-0190d78ec97b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404025} {:answer/id #uuid "46477b31-f16d-5120-a625-74e00bf51669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404026} {:answer/id #uuid "eb064b01-f667-53e4-8106-1aaf96595200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404027} {:answer/id #uuid "23bd81ec-466e-5b37-9878-ddbec03eee54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46477b31-f16d-5120-a625-74e00bf51669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404026} :answer-field/id #uuid "6704063a-555e-57cc-88b9-c16e0fa9f2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc9954e3-432a-5a64-87f7-a9d25a9f95d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79301" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3bb8c00ab2e0ef3e7c1bc21726458e718e67897f28093321884bd1c67ca768f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-5$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6e417b-e2a7-57f1-8dcd-4de5ff5d7e6b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Inversely Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967d9b2c-1fda-53bc-ae0d-e10db710fdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404031} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ea423a-8ac0-584d-a9cf-f4c2ccea48eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{T},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404032} {:answer/id #uuid "021d55c8-03a2-563f-ad3a-db919a1e2246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404033} {:answer/id #uuid "e4736ffb-9caa-5548-a49e-35adb9ed2dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{3},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404034} {:answer/id #uuid "313b345a-3675-5490-b55d-49f946a9d2d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{\\sqrt[3]{T}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021d55c8-03a2-563f-ad3a-db919a1e2246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404033} :answer-field/id #uuid "54fb041f-00b7-53a7-b2c7-5721abae068d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e640b0e6-4f4b-5358-84ca-251a991cee48" :question/math-academy-id "q-79306" :question/problem "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a hot drink decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the cube of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the drink $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a hot drink **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **cube of the temperature** $T.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *cube of the temperature.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72faae25-5e2c-5641-a31e-198f13b85833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s-1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404038} {:answer/id #uuid "60fd44b5-40fc-5e2e-ba3b-d25072a0023b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404039} {:answer/id #uuid "d44ae010-dbbe-5f4d-b0e4-19ecdfbf0caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404040} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d0ddbf-ea24-5c90-92e4-c9ae79a6aded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-1}{{s}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404041} {:answer/id #uuid "c8338925-abcb-5816-8940-b12198bb3d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8338925-abcb-5816-8940-b12198bb3d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404042} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0d8007-7775-5b7a-a407-ec01a5ac1b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9e7904b-a424-5faa-b622-0f16d31ff921" :question/math-academy-id "q-79438" :question/problem "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+L{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}+3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c29ea0e-f357-5dc3-99ca-2bae8aec29b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404045} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ea6ef9-6b75-5257-b121-1b523e9b915b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404046} {:answer/id #uuid "666c638e-5bf2-5c76-ab61-9718d548bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404047} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7dd162-1ea2-5c31-abfa-4c05e5a985ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404048} {:answer/id #uuid "ac02fad1-6d75-529b-b2da-c5e1cd09aae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "666c638e-5bf2-5c76-ab61-9718d548bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404047} :answer-field/id #uuid "040589f2-2b68-5371-a3aa-d45a057e519d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26800737-1d20-5fc8-93e8-d8bcbcdef142" :question/math-academy-id "q-79577" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =2(tx)(ty)(tz)-(ty)^{3}+(ty)^{2}(tz) \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}xyz-{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{y}^{2}z \\\\  & ={t}^{3}(2xyz-{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}z) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec0bd00-6c95-5ad2-9a20-e6fa27f6b7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404052} {:answer/id #uuid "4027ffef-21bf-5026-a35d-cacbaeea7718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404053} {:answer/id #uuid "377531b4-668f-592e-8418-7d547f543a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404054} {:answer/id #uuid "37f0ffe2-851a-513c-8df5-26662dc4ef64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404055} {:answer/id #uuid "0646923e-bb24-5a58-bdd6-f6f8d6b30748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0646923e-bb24-5a58-bdd6-f6f8d6b30748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404056} :answer-field/id #uuid "a78239aa-6f5c-55b1-bce4-31c264920ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25cb2680-651e-5a50-81aa-a03a420f17ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-79609" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax{y}_{1}-bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\na{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})=a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee4f807-4aa5-5a16-8059-65d96394945a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404059} {:answer/id #uuid "223caf6f-af28-5ef9-9a4f-1a520db495e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404060} {:answer/id #uuid "337814ed-5fcc-59cf-8f04-ff8ec9f234ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404061} {:answer/id #uuid "7a73c3ff-fb2c-5b83-a30f-1c7130a2b411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404062} {:answer/id #uuid "41ee67ee-bf37-531f-ae68-93bf2d23f87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{8}+C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ee4f807-4aa5-5a16-8059-65d96394945a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404059} :answer-field/id #uuid "8853e332-7fcd-583c-9b4c-e2dadf8be7ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "144b4980-16bd-56a0-bd2d-6e2904dde295" :question/math-academy-id "q-79784" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8x{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{4}{x}y\\,,\\,y>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1/2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2{y}^{1/2}}\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}=2z\\frac{dz}{dx}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8xz-\\frac{4}{x}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-\\frac{2}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+C)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfbba14c-2f05-5726-a785-65e6001ed044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404066} {:answer/id #uuid "4c881cec-e1cd-5edf-9137-3cff2e33554f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404067} {:answer/id #uuid "641040d1-65b8-5f54-96f4-8c708a40416c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404068} {:answer/id #uuid "3591323b-6891-5cae-8d4e-c948451b3786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404069} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f217b-b9d6-5f00-901a-4222c7e159a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c881cec-e1cd-5edf-9137-3cff2e33554f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404067} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae248aa2-3ead-50b7-9e6b-de77efc79d9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "742f0d7e-0be6-5200-afaf-b211266d03e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79799" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\nII. $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\nIII. $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}=\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{e}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{e},{c}_{2}=0.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because ${e}^{-4t}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=0,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a3eb10-2e29-538b-8e42-ae99c94ac32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404073} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb2d24f-0cf5-5987-8f28-2e07f5e68a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404074} {:answer/id #uuid "a90d16a2-31d8-5954-91c9-74ac65bc33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd772fdb-05d3-5e4f-8029-e1013c88e05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x-1)^{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404076} {:answer/id #uuid "845d8cbd-0461-59c4-ad0c-0c173f3eec19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x-1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90d16a2-31d8-5954-91c9-74ac65bc33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404075} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5400590-876e-51ce-80d8-59c049ac6615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2213d2c-5fa6-5310-a52f-41ff5bbd4ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79847" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n(x+1)\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{y}^{1/2}(x+1)^{3}+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,x>-1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{1/2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(0)=\\sqrt{y(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1},\\,Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int 1/(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)^{-1}} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>-1.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=(x+1)^{-1}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,z & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ z & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(0)=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(0) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (0+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{0}^{2}+0+C) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz=(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f407cd0-11c1-59c6-a7c5-25e4be37e2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a First-Order Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afa0a96-1e18-57e5-96cd-8e8e36f2fa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404080} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7b5a8b-e6fb-5d55-8fd3-3aca05a0f8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404081} {:answer/id #uuid "56264eb4-a51b-5aab-ba16-b250b94b35ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404082} {:answer/id #uuid "44cffaf9-c852-530e-9f05-aa73eb3e2216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404083} {:answer/id #uuid "db0e845a-af2d-57ff-ad0f-ce6c7a19a615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56264eb4-a51b-5aab-ba16-b250b94b35ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a9402c-24fc-5626-9974-b4187437af29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bddd3bab-9ed7-5695-ab3a-94854e4c62de" :question/math-academy-id "q-79950" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=yx+{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=0,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =y(0)(0)+{e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)={y}^{′}x+y+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)(0)+y(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{″}x+{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}={y}^{″}x+2{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{″}(0)(0)+2{y}^{′}(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =0+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 3+⋯ \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f407cd0-11c1-59c6-a7c5-25e4be37e2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a First-Order Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848ad199-1e25-5c94-a266-2a096cb64c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404087} {:answer/id #uuid "3c14f5c5-8ea4-5bf2-8134-553a460b1e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404088} {:answer/id #uuid "2328a7d3-d4ee-5a30-ad0f-222ab3e91a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404089} {:answer/id #uuid "c15ca29a-9669-5876-aeb2-1a4d68c21031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404090} {:answer/id #uuid "48088ea4-10b5-580c-ba68-2cb63181cdf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c15ca29a-9669-5876-aeb2-1a4d68c21031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404090} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b2114bc-ceff-5608-a240-0cb6adf8c6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13dde15f-53dc-52e9-8781-7f7c083b83f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79951" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2(0)-y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2-{y}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2-{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =2-(1)(1)+(-1)(0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=-{y}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-{y}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =-{y}^{″}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-(1)(1)+2(1)(0)+(-1)(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-2)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df3b7c7-8ab5-56e8-8b9c-3e3f6b261779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404094} {:answer/id #uuid "bb986d69-47f8-539e-8e78-570956df6719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404095} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7bfd3a-84c3-5bd7-86de-43e46a13b773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404096} {:answer/id #uuid "72889b64-0d8f-5cf5-a078-304cd0270229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404097} {:answer/id #uuid "d85b9e14-5285-547f-8225-bac68ffb2c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d85b9e14-5285-547f-8225-bac68ffb2c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404098} :answer-field/id #uuid "9085b031-ef66-5fae-9905-de9a5b2e5bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e995591-79aa-5800-bd07-e99e51f5d9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80373" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0eb10f-8358-5f70-9dcf-5a12623a7ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404101} {:answer/id #uuid "1df643a8-80e1-5748-9179-fabd113dde47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404102} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd75f60-e366-51aa-9704-98e0a480bbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404103} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404104} {:answer/id #uuid "f62ab074-e099-5956-af5c-d32875cc38df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404104} :answer-field/id #uuid "76990587-1106-5838-a7d3-5abb529f309f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb53181-29d9-5f89-8598-3c491720e6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80389" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404108} {:answer/id #uuid "c73ad559-0eae-5f15-bdf7-739e613b69ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404109} {:answer/id #uuid "d0038efe-a96a-5281-ad36-c4af7f9c03a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404110} {:answer/id #uuid "c0406221-3155-5859-8236-e58b9f481700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404111} {:answer/id #uuid "d939f8e6-1f46-548c-8adc-229c62fd7d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b352245b-eaa4-5fde-aea9-5afac2d00b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84388249-87e9-543c-a81f-fd99783cee24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15560ba1-c0e7-57a3-bd24-dc19e7e9f52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404115} {:answer/id #uuid "4a752dce-688f-5fd3-becb-69a53449004c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404116} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bff80f-44fe-590e-825f-851b2661193c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404117} {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404118} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20c357-a80f-5d68-9f17-47dfa214c55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404118} :answer-field/id #uuid "958185d9-bfb3-5c99-9580-566f3546885e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7be4462e-3d0b-5745-b83e-db8e5d74f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80493" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
